course: dictionary
lesson: 12282
title: Words for getting better
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Convalescence is the slow time of getting better after an illness or an operation. The person is no longer very ill, but they are still weak and need rest, good food and time before they can do everything again.
explain: Read the clues in the sentence: «After his operation, Sam had a long convalescence at home, resting in bed.» The words operation, resting and getting better tell you that convalescence is a time of healing, not a time of being ill with no hope.
explain: Try the swap check. Put "the time of getting better" in place of convalescence: «After his operation, Sam had a long time of getting better at home.» It still makes sense, so you have found its meaning.
fact: Convalescence means the time of slowly getting your strength back after an illness or operation.
fact: «During her convalescence, Aunt Rose could not climb the stairs yet.» Feeling weak is normal during convalescence.
fact: Useful phrases are «a long convalescence», «during his convalescence» and «a period of convalescence».
fact: Convalescence is a noun, and it is usually not counted, so you say «some convalescence» rather than «three convalescences».
fact: Convalescence is different from being ill: the illness is over, but the person still needs to rest.
exercise: «After his operation, Sam had a long convalescence at home.» What does convalescence mean here? | The start of an illness | A new kind of bicycle | A school trip to the seaside | *The time of getting your strength back after an illness
exercise: «During her convalescence, Aunt Rose could not climb the stairs yet.» What does this tell you about Aunt Rose? | She is running a race | The illness is still getting worse | She has finished all her chores | *She is still weak, but getting better
exercise: Ella asks how to check the meaning of convalescence in «a period of convalescence». What is a good way? | Read the sentence aloud twice only | Ignore the word, it is not important | *Put in "a time of getting better" and see if the sentence still makes sense | Guess a colour
test: Tom had flu last week. He feels weak now, but he is well enough to go out slowly and rest a lot. What is Tom in? | Race | Holiday | Surgery | *Convalescence
test: Which sentence uses convalescence correctly? | «She ran a convalescence in the park.» | *«She spent a long convalescence at home after her operation.» | «He drank a convalescence of orange juice.» | «The convalescence kicked the ball over the fence.»
exam: What does convalescence mean? | The illness that makes you very ill | A kind of fast running race | *The time of slowly getting your strength back after an illness or operation | A doctor who works in a hospital
